$9$ points are marked in a plane. Exactly $4$ of the points lie on a straight line, and no other set of $3$ points is collinear.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0.2,3.0) -- (5.4,3.0);
\fill (0.8,3.0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$A$};
\fill (2.1,3.0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$B$};
\fill (3.4,3.0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$C$};
\fill (4.8,3.0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$D$};
\fill (1.1,1.3) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$E$};
\fill (2.8,1.0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$F$};
\fill (3.8,1.4) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$G$};
\fill (2.3,4.3) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$H$};
\fill (4.9,5.1) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$I$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
How many distinct triangles can be formed with vertices at these points?
